# Trivy findings that are accepted rather than fixed.
#
# The pipeline validates this file before it will even start a scan: every entry
# needs, in the contiguous comment block directly above it, a line matching
#   expires YYYY-MM-DD
# An expired entry fails the build, which is the point -- an exception here is a
# dated decision, not a way to quiet a scan.

# DS-0002 | Dockerfile | akzeptiert 2026-09-09 | expires 2027-03-01
#
# "Specify at least 1 USER command in Dockerfile with non-root user as argument."
#
# Accepted, because it does not describe this image. The container starts as
# root ON PURPOSE and drops to uid 10001 inside the binary (dropPrivileges in
# cmd/uploader/privdrop_linux.go, called first thing in runServe). It has to:
# a bind-mounted ./data/files is created on the host as root, so the runtime
# user cannot create the shard directories it writes blobs into and every
# upload would fail with a 500. Doing the drop in the binary is also what lets
# the runtime image ship without su-exec, and therefore without apk packages.
#
# A `USER uploader` line would turn this rule green and break the ownership
# fixup -- a measurably worse image for a passing check. The process that
# actually serves traffic is unprivileged either way, which is what the rule is
# trying to ensure.
#
# Re-evaluate by the expiry date, or sooner if storage stops needing the chown.
DS-0002
